00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:58.400
Our AI analysis is learning from the volume confirmation pattern and adjusting position sizing on

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:05.260
momentum setups accordingly. Imagine you bought an ETF tracking Canadian banks last fall

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:14.700
and it is now down 12% in your TFSA. You sell to lock in the loss for tax purposes but the superficial

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:21.340
loss rule means you cannot repurchase the identical ETF for 30 days or the loss is denied.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:29.980
The mechanism works like this. The 30-day window applies to the same security or one the CRA considers

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:38.820
identical so switching to a similar but not identical broad market ETF for example moving from XIU to

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:47.260
XIC lets you keep market exposure while claiming the capital loss. A pro tip most retail investors

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:55.480
miss is that losses can be carried forward indefinitely and offset future gains and the new 50% capital gains

00:09:55.480 --> 00:10:02.380
inclusion rate makes harvesting even more valuable because every dollar of loss now shelters more

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:11.080
taxable income. The T50008 slip your broker issues will report the proceeds so track your adjusted cost

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:17.980
base carefully in a spreadsheet to avoid double counting. The biggest mistake with tax loss harvesting

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:25.740
is selling and immediately buying back the exact same ticker. Instead always map to a close substitute

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:32.760
and set a calendar reminder for the 31st day to decide whether to switch back. Before we wrap

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:40.400
open your brokerage app tomorrow morning and check the distance of any Canadian equity ETF to its 200-day

